==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ang|þinċan}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*þunkijaną}}. Cognate with {{cog|osx|thunkian}}, {{cog|goh|dunken}}, {{cog|non|þykkja}}, {{cog|got|𐌸𐌿𐌲𐌺𐌾𐌰𐌽}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|þynċan}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb|þynċan}}

# to [[seem]], ({{glossary|impersonal}}, with {{glossary|dative case|dative}} {{glossary|subject}}) to [[think]]
#* '''c. 990''', ''{{w|Wessex Gospels}}'', [https://archive.org/stream/dahalgangodspelo00thor#page/n211/mode/2up John 8:53]
#*: {{quote|ang|Hwæt '''þyncþ''' þē þæt þū sīe?|Who do you '''think''' you are?}}
#* '''late 9th century''', [[w:Alfred the Great|King Alfred's]] [https://books.google.com/books?id=8sy8OOmW-ZAC&pg=PA119 translation] of [[w:Boethius|Boethius']] ''{{w|The Consolation of Philosophy}}''
#*: {{quote|ang|Hwȳ ne sċolde mē swā '''þynċan'''?|Why shouldn't I '''think''' so?}}
#* '''c. 992''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://books.google.com/books?id=m2hVAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA308 "The Saints Alexander, Eventius, and Theodosius"]
#*: {{quote|ang|Þā cwæþ Aureliānus, "Eom iċ hund '''ġeþūht'''?"|Then Aurelianus said, "Do I '''look like''' a dog?"}}
#* '''late 9th century''', [[w:Alfred the Great|King Alfred's]] [https://books.google.com/books?id=8sy8OOmW-ZAC&pg=PA44 translation] of [[w:Boethius|Boethius']] ''{{w|The Consolation of Philosophy}}''
#*: {{quote|ang|Tīen þūsenda ġēara, þēah hit lang '''þynċe''', āsċortaþ.|Ten thousand years, even if it '''seems''' long, runs out.}}
#* '''c. 992''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://books.google.com/books?id=2xobVW92JLcC&pg=PA376 "On the Passion of the Apostles Peter and Paul"]
#*: {{quote|ang|Simōn bræġd his hīw ætforan þām cāsere, swā þæt hē wearþ fǣrlīċe '''ġeþūht''' cnapa, and eft hārwenġe.|Simon changed his appearance in front of the emperor, so that he suddenly '''looked like''' a boy, then like a bearded old man.}}
#* '''late 10th century''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], ''[[w:Old English Hexateuch|the Old English Hexateuch]]'', [https://books.google.com/books?id=QrZUAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA72 Genesis 29:20]
#*: {{quote|ang|Iacōb him hīersumode seofon ġēar for Rācēle, and hit '''þūhte''' him fēawa daga for þǣre lufe þe hē tō hire hæfde.|Jacob served him for seven years for Rachael, but it '''seemed''' to him like a few days because of the love that he had for her.}}
#* '''c. 992''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://books.google.com/books?id=m2hVAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA382 "On the Festival of St. Peter the Apostle"]
#*: {{quote|ang|Sē enġel cwæþ, "Begyrd þē and sċō þē and fylġ mē." Petrus þā him fylġde, and '''þūhte''' him swelċe hit swefn wǣre.|The angel said, "Put on your belt and shoes and follow me." Peter followed him, and it '''felt''' to him like he was dreaming.}}
#* '''c. 990''', ''{{w|Wessex Gospels}}'', [https://archive.org/stream/dahalgangodspelo00thor#page/n63/mode/2up Matthew 23:27]
#*: {{quote|ang|Ġē sind ġelīċe hwītum byrġenum, þā '''þynċaþ''' mannum ūtan wlitiga, and hīe sind innan fulla dēadra bāna and ealre fȳlðe.|You're like whitewashed tombs, which '''look''' beautiful from the outside, while on the inside they're full of dead people's bones and all kinds of filth.}}
#* '''c. 994''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://books.google.com/books?id=eDdECgAAQBAJ&pg=PA161 "Preface to Genesis"]
#*: {{quote|ang|Þā unġelǣredan prēostas, ġif hīe hwæt lȳtles understandaþ of þām Lǣdenbōcum, þonne '''þyncþ''' him sōna þæt hīe mæġen mǣre lārēowas bēon.|If uneducated priests understand anything at all from the Latin texts, they immediately '''think''' they can be famous teachers.}}
#* '''c. 992''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Homilies_of_the_Anglo-Saxon_Church/VI "The Octaves and Circumcision of our Lord"]
#*: {{quote|ang|Þa gesceafta ðe sind þwyrlice '''ġeðūhte''', hí sind to wrace gesceapene yfel-dædum.|The creatures that are '''thought''' monstrous have been created for punishment of evil deeds.}}
#* '''late 10th century''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://archive.org/details/aelfricslivesof01aelf/page/522/mode/2up "The Seven Sleepers"]
#*: {{quote|ang|...and he, malchus, þa him swa wundorlice '''þuhte''' eall þæt he geseah and gehyrde...|And he, Malchus, when everything that he saw and heard '''seemed''' so wonderful to him,...}}
# to [[appear]]
#* '''c. 992''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Homilies_of_the_Anglo-Saxon_Church/XXXVIII "The Nativity of St. Andrew the Apostle"]
#*: {{quote|ang|Gif se Hælend gecure æt fruman getinge láreowas, and woruldlice uðwitan, and ðyllice to bodigenne sende, þonne wære '''geðuht''' swilce se soða geleafa ne asprunge ðurh Godes mihte, ac of woruldlicere getingnysse.|f Jesus had chosen at first eloquent teachers, and sent worldly philosophers, and the like to preach, then would it have '''appeared''' as if the true faith had not sprung up through God's might, but from worldly eloquence.}}

====Usage notes====
* The {{glossary|impersonal}} use "to think" refers to thinking as in having an opinion. For thinking as in having thoughts, {{m|ang|þenċan}} is used.

